      "locked": false,
      "text": "Graph Theory\n\nTypes of graphs based on the edges:\n\n1) Undirected Graph: Every edge is undirected\n2) Directed Graph: Every edge is directed\n3) Multigraph: Undirected Graph. Multiedges(same edge used more than one time between same pair of nodes) allowed, but self-loops not allowed\n4) Pseudo Graph: Undirected Graph. Multiedges and self loops allowed. (ANY UNDIRECTED GRAPH)\n5) Directed Multigraph: multiedges and self loops allowed. (ANY DIRECTED GRAPH)\n6) Simple Graph: Undirected Graph with no self loops and multi-edges.\n7) Simple Directed Graph: No self loops and multi-edges.\n\nFor any Undirected Graph: \n\nIsolated Vertex - Degree 0.  Pendant Vertex - Degree 1.  A self loop contrbutes 2 to the degree of the vertex.\nDEGREE SEQUENCE of a graph is the n-term sequence of degrees of each vertex written in descending order of the vertex degrees.\n-> ORDER  of a graph is the cardinality of it's Vertex Set. SIZE of a graph is the cardinality of it's edge set.\n-> Handshaking theorem: Total Degree = 2 * |E|\n-> In any graph, number of odd-degree vertices is always even.\n\n\nFor any Directed Graph:\n\n-> Indegree: of a vertex is the number of edges where the vertex is the second element.\n-> Outdegree: of a vertex is the number of edges where the vertex is the first element.\n-> Total Indegree = Total Outdegree = Size of Graph = Number of edges in graph.\n\n\nFor any Undirected Graph:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n-> A cycle Must have atleast 3 distinct vertices, since it is a path.\n-> If there is a WALK from v to w, then there is a PATH from v to w.\n-> Connected Graph: A graph G is connected iff there is a path b/w every two vertices.\n-> Distance: b/w two vertices a and b is the length of the shortest path b/w a and b.\n-> Diameter: of a CONNECTED graph is the maximum distance among all the distances between every pair of nodes.\n   So, in a graph with Diameter D, distance b/w any pair of nodes is always less than or equal to D.\n   Diameter of a disconnected graph is Undefined/Infinite.\n\n\nTypes of graphs for undirected graphs:\n\n1) Regular Graph: The degree of all vertices is same.\n   -> For a d-regular graph:\n    - Degree Sequence is d repeated n times.\n    - Avg degree = min degree = max degree = d\n    - Total degree = nd\n    - # edges = nd/2\n    - May be connected or disconnected\n    - Diameter may be finite or infinite.\n   \n   -> 3-regular graph is CUBIC.\n    \n\n2) Complete Graph (Kn) : is a simple graph that contains exactly one edge between each pair of distinct vertices.\n    - Degree of every vertex is n-1. So it is a n-1 Regular Graph\n    - has n(n-1)/2 edges\n    - Diameter is 1\n    - Always Connected\n\n3) Empty/Null/Edgeless Graph(En): A graph with no edges. just n isolated vertices.\n    - 0 regular graph\n    - Diameter is 1 iff n = 1, infinte otherwise\n    - Connected iff n = 1, Disconnected otherwise\n\n4) Path Graph(Pn): is a simple graph and is just a straight line.\n    - Degree of first and last vertex is 1, and the rest have 2.\n    - Connected.\n    - Diameter = n-1\n    - #edges = n-1\n    - Total degree = 2(n-1)\n\n5) Cycle Graph(Cn): a Path graph with an edge from last vertex to first vertex.\n    - is a 2-regular graph\n    - Connected\n    - Diameter = floor(n/2)\n    - # edges = n\n\n6) Wheel Graph: is a Cycle graph with an additional vertex that has an edge to all the other vertices.\n    - Total degree = 4*(n-1)\n    - Max degree = n-1\n    - min degree = 3\n    - # edges = 2*(n-1)\n    - Connected\n    - Diameter = 2 iff n > 4, 1 otherwise.\n\n    This is when we consider n as the TOTAL NUMBER OF NODES. But there is popular notation Wn, where n represents \n    the number of OUTER nodes. i.e. it excludes the EXTRA node that is added to the cycle graph.\n\n7) Hypercube Graph(Qn): is a simple graph with 2^n nodes, where each node represents an n-length bit strings.\n    And there is an edge from one node to the other, iff the hamming distance between the bit strings represented by the nodes is 1.\n    \n    For an n-cube graph:\n        - n regular graph\n        - 2^n nodes\n        - Total degree = n * 2^n\n        - #edges = n * 2^n / 2\n        - Diameter = n\n        - Connected\n\n    This is the same structure as \"Boolean Lattice\".\n\n\nSubgraph:\n\n-> G1 is a subgraph of G2 iff V1 is a subset of V2 and E1 is a subset of E2.\n\n-> Spanning Subgraph: G1 is a spanning subgraph of G2, iff V1 = V2 and E1 is a subset of E2.\n\n-> Induced Subgraph: G1 is an induced subgraph of G2, iff V1 is a subset of V2 and every edge b/w vertices of V1 in G2 is also present in G1\n\n-> The original graph itself is the only subgraph which is both spanning and induced.\n\n\nGraph Isomorphism:\n\n-> Two Graphs G1 and G2 are isomorphic iff there exists a bijection b/w V1 and V2 which preserves\n    adjacency.\n-> i.e. for all a,b in V1 ((a,b) E e1 <-> (f(a), f(b)) E e2). The bijection f is called an isomorphism.\n-> Intuitively, two graphs are isomorphic if they are structurally identical - meaning one graph can be transformed into the other simply by\n    relabelling its vertices or moving them around without tearing or adding any edges.\n\nIf two graphs are isomorphic there are some properties that are preserved, called \"Graph Invariants or Isomorphic Invariants\".\nThese are:\n\n1) Vertex and Edge Counts\n2) Degree Sequence\n3) Connected Components\n4) Subgraphs and Cycles\n5) Spectral Properties: Their adjacency matrices share the exact same eigenvalues.  \n\nINVARIANTS ARE NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT.\n\nProving two graphs are ISOMORPHIC is expensive (NP - Intermediate). But we can disprove it by checking the invariant properties.\nProcedure to disprove isomorphism:\n- Connectedness\n- Check Order and Size of Graphs\n- Min Degree, Max Degree\n- Degree Sequence\n- Number of 3 -length cycles to 5-length cycles\n- Diameter\n\n-> Given a set of all undirected simple graphs on n nodes. A relation R on the set, defined such that two graphs G and G' are related if there is\n   a bijection f from the vertices of G to the vertices of G' such that (u,v) is an edge in G iff (f(u), f(v)) is an edge in G'. Then,\n    R is an EQUIVALENCE Relation.\n\n\nComplement of a Graph:\n\n-> Given a graph G(V, E) where |V| = n, the complement graph of it is G'(V, E'), where E' = Edges in Kn - E.\n-> |E| + |E'| = nC2.\n-> A graph G is called a SELF COMPLEMENTARY GRAPH iff G and G' are isomorphic. => nC2 = 2e\n\n\nConnected Component: of a graph G is a connected subgraph of G that is not contained in any other connected subgraph of G. In other words, it is a maximal connected subgraph.\n-> The relation on vertices, R, such that aRb iff there is a path between a and b is an equivalence relation, where each equivalence class is a component.\n\nG_{n,p} is almost surely connected if p > log(n)/n.\n\n-> Complement of a disconnected graph is always a connected graph\n-> If only two vertices have an odd degree then they must be in the same component.\n\n\nBipartite Graph: a Simple graph is a bipartite graph if its vertext set V can be partitioined into two disjoint sets V1 and V2 such that\nevery edge in the graph connects a vertex in V1 and a vertex in V2. We call (V1, V2) a BIPARTITION of V.\nTotal Degree of V1 = Total Degree of V2\n\n-> Pn is Bipartite.\n-> Cn is Bipartite. iff n is even.\n-> Only K1 and K2 are bipartite\n-> G is bipartite iff there is no odd lengthed cycle in G.\n-> If G is a k-regular bipartite graph with k > 0 and the bipartition of G is V1 and V2, then |V1| = |V2|\n-> Subgraph of a Bipartite Graph is also bipartite.\n\n\nComplete Bipartite Graph(Km, n): If every vertex in V1 is adjacent to every vertex in V2. K(1, n) is called \"Star Graph\"\n        Diameter of K(1, 1) is 1. for K(m,n) it is 2.\n\nIf a graph has n vertices and more than (n/2)^2 edges, then it cannot be a BIPARTITE\n\n\nCyclic Graph: There are atleast two vertices which have more than one path between them.\nAcyclic Graph: There is atmost one path between any pair of vertices.\n\nForest: is an Acyclic Graph.\nTree: is a connected Acyclic Graph.\n\nForest. b/w any two vertices we have ATMOST ONE path.\nTree. b/w any two vertices we have EXACTLY ONE path.\n\nMaximally Acylclic: G is acyclic, G + uv is cyclic\nMinimally Connected: G is connected, G - e is disconnected.\n\n-> Connected Graph with n vertices and n-1 edges is a TREE.\n-> Maximally Acyclic Graph on n vertices is a TREE.\n-> Minimally Connected Graph on n vertices is a TREE.\n\nEVERY TREE WITH ATLEAST TWO VERTICES HAS ATLEAST TWO VETICES WITH DEGREE 1.\n\n\nRooted Tree:\n    - is a Directed Acyclic Graph\n    - Unique root.\n    - Unique path from root to every other vertex.\n\nHeight of each node: is the length of the longest path from current node to any leaf.\nDepth of each node: is the length of the path from root to current node.\nDegree of a node is the number of children of the node.\n\n\nBinary Tree: is a rooted Tree with every node having atmost 2 children.\nFull binary Tree: is a binary Tree with every non-leaf node having exactly two children.\n\n-> In any binary Tree, number of Leaves = number of nodes with 2 children + 1",
