      "text": "Graph Numbers\n\nMaximal Set: A set S is maximal with respect to property P, if nothing new can be added to S, keeping the property P preserved.\nMaximum Set: A set S is maximum with respect to property P, if it is the largest possible set that preserves the property P.\n\nMinimal Set: A set S is minimal with respect to property P, if nothing can be removed from S, keeping the property P preserved.\nMinimum Set: A set S is minimum with respect to property P, if it is the smallest possible set that preserves the property P.\n\nMaximum Set is Maximal. Maximal need not be Maximum.\nMinimum Set is Minimal. Minimal need not be Minimum.\n\n1) Clique: In a graph, a set of pairwise adjacent vertices is called a CLIQUE. The size of a maximum\n   clique of G is called the CLIQUE NUMBER of G and is denoted by w(G).\n\n   -> So, a clique is a set of vertices that induces a subgraph from G, which is COMPLETE.\n\n2) Independent Set: In a graph, a set of pairwise non-adjancent vertices is called an INDEPENDENT SET. The size of a maximum\n    independent set of G is called the STABILITY NUMBER/INDEPENDENCE NUMBER and is denoted by α(G).\n\n    -> So, a Independent set induces a subgraph from G, that is EDGELESS.\n\n\nINDEPENDENT SET IN G IS THE CLIQUE IN G' AND VICE-VERSA\n\n3) Vertex Cover: is a set S of vertices of G that contains at least one endpoint of every edge of F. The vertices in S cover G.\n    -> The size of minimum vertex cover is called VERTEX COVERING NUMBER and is deonted by B(G).\n\n4) Edge Cover: is a set S of edges of G that contain every vertex of G as an endpoint.\n    -> The size of minimum edge cover is called EDGE COVERING NUMBER and is denoted by B'(G). B'(G) >= ceil(n/2)\n\n-> Covering number of Graph usually mean Vertex Covering number.\n-> The vertex with maximum degree NEED NOT BE in Minimum Vertex Cover.\n\nIF S IS A VERTEX COVER, THEN V-S IS AN INDEPENDENT SET AND VICE-VERSA.\n\n\n5) Matching: A set of pairwise non-adjacent edges in a graph is called a MATCHING. The maximum number of \n    edges in a matching in a graph G is called the MATCHING NUMBER of G and denoted by α'(G) or u(G)\n\n    -> A Matching is called PERFECT, if it covers all vertices of the graph.\n    -> A vertext that appears in a matching is SATURATED, otherwise it is UNSATURATED.\n    -> A Perfect matching in a graph is a matching that saturates every vertex.\n    -> Perfect Matching can only occur when the number of vertices is EVEN. But this is not a Sufficient condition.\n    -> Perfect Matching is Maximum. But maximum or maximal need not be Perfect Matchings.\n\n    -> u(G) <= floor(n/2)\n    -> u(G) <= B(G) <= 2*u(G)\n    -> u(G) + B'(G) = n, if no isolated vertices\n    -> In a Biparitie Graph K(m,n), u(G) = B(G) = min(m,n)\n    -> α(G) <= B'(G), if no isolated vertices\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Graph Numbers\n\nMaximal Set: A set S is maximal with respect to property P, if nothing new can be added to S, keeping the property P preserved.\nMaximum Set: A set S is maximum with respect to property P, if it is the largest possible set that preserves the property P.\n\nMinimal Set: A set S is minimal with respect to property P, if nothing can be removed from S, keeping the property P preserved.\nMinimum Set: A set S is minimum with respect to property P, if it is the smallest possible set that preserves the property P.\n\nMaximum Set is Maximal. Maximal need not be Maximum.\nMinimum Set is Minimal. Minimal need not be Minimum.\n\n1) Clique: In a graph, a set of pairwise adjacent vertices is called a CLIQUE. The size of a maximum\n   clique of G is called the CLIQUE NUMBER of G and is denoted by w(G).\n\n   -> So, a clique is a set of vertices that induces a subgraph from G, which is COMPLETE.\n\n2) Independent Set: In a graph, a set of pairwise non-adjancent vertices is called an INDEPENDENT SET. The size of a maximum\n    independent set of G is called the STABILITY NUMBER/INDEPENDENCE NUMBER and is denoted by α(G).\n\n    -> So, a Independent set induces a subgraph from G, that is EDGELESS.\n\n\nINDEPENDENT SET IN G IS THE CLIQUE IN G' AND VICE-VERSA\n\n3) Vertex Cover: is a set S of vertices of G that contains at least one endpoint of every edge of F. The vertices in S cover G.\n    -> The size of minimum vertex cover is called VERTEX COVERING NUMBER and is deonted by B(G).\n\n4) Edge Cover: is a set S of edges of G that contain every vertex of G as an endpoint.\n    -> The size of minimum edge cover is called EDGE COVERING NUMBER and is denoted by B'(G). B'(G) >= ceil(n/2)\n\n-> Covering number of Graph usually mean Vertex Covering number.\n-> The vertex with maximum degree NEED NOT BE in Minimum Vertex Cover.\n\nIF S IS A VERTEX COVER, THEN V-S IS AN INDEPENDENT SET AND VICE-VERSA.\n\n\n5) Matching: A set of pairwise non-adjacent edges in a graph is called a MATCHING. The maximum number of \n    edges in a matching in a graph G is called the MATCHING NUMBER of G and denoted by α'(G) or u(G)\n\n    -> A Matching is called PERFECT, if it covers all vertices of the graph.\n    -> A vertext that appears in a matching is SATURATED, otherwise it is UNSATURATED.\n    -> A Perfect matching in a graph is a matching that saturates every vertex.\n    -> Perfect Matching can only occur when the number of vertices is EVEN. But this is not a Sufficient condition.\n    -> Perfect Matching is Maximum. But maximum or maximal need not be Perfect Matchings.\n\n    -> u(G) <= floor(n/2)\n    -> u(G) <= B(G) <= 2*u(G)\n    -> u(G) + B'(G) = n, if no isolated vertices\n    -> In a Biparitie Graph K(m,n), u(G) = B(G) = min(m,n)\n    -> α(G) <= B'(G), if no isolated vertices\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
